  ('7b176df6-cdc7-5645-af09-6e7a093553af', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'proportional-reasoning'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Each table below uses the same fractional $x$ values. In which table is $y$ proportional to $x$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x$: $\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{3}{2}$, $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y$: $\\frac{1}{3}$, $1$, $\\frac{5}{3}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$x$: $\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{3}{2}$, $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y$: $1$, $2$, $3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"constant-difference-not-ratio"},{"text":"$x$: $\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{3}{2}$, $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y$: $\\frac{1}{4}$, $\\frac{3}{4}$, $\\frac{3}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"checked-only-first-two-pairs"},{"text":"$x$: $\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{3}{2}$, $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y$: $2$, $\\frac{2}{3}$, $\\frac{2}{5}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"constant-product-is-proportional"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x$: $\frac{1}{2}$, $\frac{3}{2}$, $\frac{5}{2}$ and $y$: $\frac{1}{3}$, $1$, $\frac{5}{3}$$q$, $q$For the table with $y$: $\frac{1}{3}$, $1$, $\frac{5}{3}$, each ratio $\frac{y}{x}$ is $\frac{2}{3}$, so it is proportional. The table with $y$: $\frac{1}{4}$, $\frac{3}{4}$, $\frac{3}{2}$ has ratios $\frac{1}{2}$, $\frac{1}{2}$ and $\frac{3}{5}$, so it breaks at the last pair. The table with $y$: $2$, $\frac{2}{3}$, $\frac{2}{5}$ has a constant product $xy = 1$, not a constant ratio.$q$, 'draft', null)
